    'section.statute.title' => 'The statutes of :org',
    'section.history.title' => 'The history',
    'section.history.h3_1' => 'Aims of the association',
    'section.history.h3_2' => 'Challenges and changes',
    'section.history.h3_3' => 'The cultural mission of the Hungarian Association',
    'section.history.h3_4' => 'The Hungarian Legion',
    'section.history.h3_5' => 'The challenges of the 20th century',
    'section.history.h3_6' => 'From 1956 to the present day',
    'section.history.p1' => 'On June 15, 1846, 29 Hungarian craftsmen gathered in a small inn at the invitation of sieve-maker Ignác Berlek from Szekszárd, locksmith István Gönczi from Miskolc and master tailor János Schuh from Veszprémpalota. They were unaware that they would become part of a historic event that day.',
    'section.history.p2' => 'Under the chairmanship of Mr. Berlek, the Hungarian Association was founded on that summer day – a significant step towards promoting cohesion and the interests of Hungarian craftsmen in Prussia.',
    'section.history.p3' => 'The minutes of the founding meeting from 175 years ago recorded the following names:',
    'section.history.p4' => 'With the statutes, which were soon put in writing and remarkably democratic for their time, 86 members in the Prussian capital with its approximately 400,000 inhabitants were already familiar by January 1847.',
    'section.history.p5' => 'Regardless of religious differences, the association was primarily committed to mutual help and support. This was set down in the "Rules of the Association for Hungarians and Transylvanians living in Berlin" in the following points:',

    'section.history.figcap_1' => 'Mózes Szakács, the first chronicler of the association',

    'section.history.list_1.1' => 'Support Hungarian and Transylvanian compatriots who come to Berlin and stay here temporarily or long-term with advice and action.',
    'section.history.list_1.2' => 'Draw travellers\' attention to Berlin\'s sights during their stay and provide financial support if necessary.',
    'section.history.list_1.3' => 'Financially support Hungarians living, staying temporarily or travelling through Berlin in case of illness and – if possible – provide care.',
    'section.history.list_1.4' => 'The members of the association shall meet regularly to cultivate friendly ties and organise joint activities.',

    'section.history.list_2.1' => 'Abroad, you represent the entire nation. Behave accordingly and bring recognition to the name of Hungary!',
    'section.history.list_2.2' => 'Be proud of your Hungarian identity! Be self-confident, but never impolite!',
    'section.history.list_2.3' => 'Support your compatriots and all matters concerning Hungary!',
    'section.history.list_2.4' => 'Respect the host nation, keep your eyes open abroad so that what you learn will benefit you at home! But do not thoughtlessly criticise conditions at home or abroad!',
    'section.history.list_2.5' => 'Do not forget that you have only one home. Elsewhere you can only ever be a guest. Ensure that your children learn the language of their homeland!',

    'section.history.p6' => 'The statutes adopted in 1847 also emphasise:',
    'section.history.p7' => 'By choosing Berlin as our permanent or temporary residence, we sons of the sister countries Hungary and Transylvania had to realise that many of our compatriots – whether apprentices or craftsmen – had to give up their goals due to economic hardship and return home. Far from home, without familiar faces, their fate was often characterised by loneliness and deprivation. Our own experience taught us how important it is to have a supportive community even abroad.',
    'section.history.p8' => 'The burgeoning Hungarian Association was considerably shaken by the political upheavals and revolutions in Europe. Almost half of the members returned to Hungary, many fought in the war of independence.',
    'section.history.p9' => 'A heated debate broke out among those remaining in Berlin over language use: while the association had hitherto been bilingual (Hungarian-German), some members advocated for the exclusive use of the Hungarian language. This regulation was not officially introduced until 1869 and enshrined in new statutes in 1886.',
    'section.history.p10' => 'In the second half of the 19th century, besides the popular balls, the number of gatherings on topics of literature, art, society and politics also increased. The Hungarian colony also subscribed to domestic newspapers and magazines – reading became increasingly popular. Thanks to Miksa Esterházy, the Hungarian Association had its own library as early as 1863. The count, who was a senior official at the Austro-Hungarian Embassy, contributed a cash donation to the purchase of the first 86 volumes. Mózes Szakács, in turn, was already writing the chronicle of the Hungarian Association in 1864.',
    'section.history.p11' => 'At the time of the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, many answered the call of Generals Klapka and Vetter, who were staying in Berlin with its 700,000 inhabitants, and joined the Hungarian Legion marching against the Austrians. The association organised a successful collection for the wounded Hungarian prisoners of war. Of course, this was neither the first nor the last time that Berlin\'s Hungarians came to the aid of their compatriots in distress. Hungarians living abroad donated to the construction of the Hungarian Academy of Sciences in Pest-Buda as well as for the flood victims in Szeged. And the list could go on...',
    'section.history.p12' => 'It is therefore understandable that interest in the activities of the Hungarian Association in Berlin grew ever greater at home. The colony also came into contact with many famous personalities. Among others, Franz Liszt, György Klapka and Mór Jókai were appointed honorary members.',
    'section.history.p13' => 'As is well known, our association was founded in the mid-19th century by people who wanted to learn the trades of tailor, carpenter, saddler, strap-maker, piano-maker, brewer, brush-maker and others in the city on the Spree and become the masters of tomorrow. By the turn of the century, the number of Hungarians living among the two million Berliners had grown considerably. The association\'s activities became so strong that its foundations could not be destroyed by either the First World War or the subsequent global economic crisis. On the contrary: arriving or travelling Hungarians continued to be helped with accommodation, food and money.',
    'section.history.p14' => 'The five admonitions from back then are still relevant today:',
    'section.history.p15' => 'Of course, children were not forgotten in the association: in cooperation with the other Hungarian colonies, the Hungarian school was founded, followed by joint events.',
    'section.history.p16' => 'The political events in Hitler\'s Germany also led to heated discussions in the Hungarian Association Berlin. The community nearly fell apart, but Ferenc Szalay, who was entrusted with the presidency, managed to restore order, so that, for example, 2200 Hungarians attended the association ball (1939).',
    'section.history.p17' => 'In 1956, on the occasion of the 110th anniversary of the association renamed the Colony, the World Federation of Hungarians awarded 14 Berlin members the Silver Wreath Badge for their dedicated work. Shortly after the anniversary celebrations, the Colony found itself in a critical situation again in connection with the 1956 events in Hungary. Once again, the association of Hungarians living in Germany\'s largest city was on the verge of collapse. However, the crisis did not last long: at the general assembly in January 1957, Ferenc Szalay was re-elected president.',
    'section.history.p18' => 'The Berlin Wall, built in 1961, divided the metropolis of 3.3 million Berliners into two parts: the Colony lost its East Berlin members but continued to exist and function. During this time, relations with the World Federation of Hungarians also developed. In addition to cultural cooperation, we also tried to provide financial help – such as for the flood victims of 1970 or in preparing Hungary for the Olympic Games in Munich, and in 1984 we also supported the SOS Children\'s Village with a transfer of 800 DM.',
    'section.history.p19' => 'From 1990, the year of German reunification, the work of the Colony was led by János Szabó, from whom Ferenc Deák then took over the presidency. The latter was succeeded in 1998 by the young Levente Földesi. Around the turn of the millennium, Mr. Szabó again took on the difficult task of leading the Colony. Thereafter, János Usztics turned his attention to rejuvenating the Colony, initially provisionally between 2001 and 2004, then permanently from the year of Hungary\'s accession to the European Union (2004). He carried out his beneficial work for more than a decade before the Colony continued actively under the leadership of János Molnár from 2015 onwards. The number of members also increased pleasingly again in the 21st century.',
    'section.history.p20' => 'János then handed over the baton in 2018 to the next president, József Robotka, who devoted himself with great enthusiasm to organising joint programmes and preparing a worthy celebration of the 175th anniversary.',
    'section.history.p21' => '',

